Kubernetes Explained: From Basics to Your Project Implementation
1. What is Kubernetes?
Kubernetes (often abbreviated as K8s) is an open-source container orchestration platform that automates:

Deployment of containerized applications

Scaling (up/down based on demand)

Management (health checks, load balancing, updates)

Why Use Kubernetes?
Runs your Docker containers efficiently in production

Handles failures automatically (self-healing)

Manages networking, storage, and security for containers

Enables zero-downtime deployments

2. Kubernetes Components Used in Your Project
Your project uses these key Kubernetes components (defined in YAML files):

a) Pod
The smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes

Contains one or more containers (your Spring Boot app runs here)

Ephemeral (temporary) – if a pod crashes, Kubernetes creates a new one

b) Deployment
Manages the lifecycle of pods (creates/replaces them)

Ensures the desired number of pods are running

Allows rolling updates (no downtime during deployments)

c) Service
Provides a stable IP/DNS name for pods (even if pods restart)

Load balances traffic between pods

Your service.yaml exposes the app to the outside world

d) PersistentVolume (PV) & PersistentVolumeClaim (PVC)
PV: Storage resource in the cluster (like a physical disk)

PVC: Request for storage by a pod (your MySQL database uses this)

Defined in mysql-pv.yaml to ensure data persists even if pods restart

3. Why YAML Files?
Kubernetes uses YAML (or JSON) files to declare:

What should be running (e.g., containers, storage)

How it should run (e.g., replicas, resource limits)

Key Benefits of YAML
Declarative Configuration: You define the desired state (Kubernetes makes it happen).

Version Control: YAML files can be tracked in Git.

Reproducibility: Same YAML = same deployment every time.

4. Your Kubernetes Files Explained
a) mysql-pv.yaml (PersistentVolume)
yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: PersistentVolume
metadata:
  name: mysql-pv
spec:
  storageClassName: manual
  capacity:
    storage: 5Gi
  accessModes:
    - ReadWriteOnce
  hostPath:
    path: "/mnt/data"
Creates a 5GB storage volume on the host machine (/mnt/data).

MySQL will store data here (so it survives pod restarts).

b) mysql-deployment.yaml (Deployment + Service)
yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: mysql
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: mysql
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: mysql
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: mysql
        image: mysql:5.7
        env:
        - name: MYSQL_ROOT_PASSWORD
          value: "password"
        ports:
        - containerPort: 3306
        volumeMounts:
        - name: mysql-storage
          mountPath: /var/lib/mysql
      volumes:
      - name: mysql-storage
        persistentVolumeClaim:
          claimName: mysql-pvc
---
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: mysql
spec:
  ports:
  - port: 3306
  selector:
    app: mysql
Deployment: Runs 1 MySQL pod with persistent storage.

Service: Allows your Spring Boot app to connect to MySQL at mysql:3306.

c) deployment.yaml (Your Spring Boot App)
yaml
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: immunization-tracker
spec:
  replicas: 1
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: immunization-tracker
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: immunization-tracker
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: app
        image: arnold21/immunizationtracker:latest
        ports:
        - containerPort: 8081
Runs 1 instance of your Spring Boot app (from your Docker image).

Listens on port 8081.

d) service.yaml (Exposes Your App)
yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Service
metadata:
  name: immunization-tracker
spec:
  type: LoadBalancer
  ports:
  - port: 80
    targetPort: 8081
  selector:
    app: immunization-tracker
Makes your app accessible outside the cluster.

Routes external traffic on port 80 to your app’s port 8081.

5. How Kubernetes Works in Your Project
Step-by-Step Flow
Jenkins applies YAML files (kubectl apply -f):

bash
kubectl apply -f mysql-pv.yaml
kubectl apply -f mysql-deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f service.yaml
Kubernetes creates resources:

PersistentVolume → MySQL Deployment → Spring Boot Deployment → Service

Your app runs:

Spring Boot connects to MySQL at mysql:3306.

Users access the app via the LoadBalancer service.

6. Key Kubernetes Commands for Debugging
Command	Purpose
kubectl get pods	List running pods
kubectl logs <pod-name>	View app logs
kubectl describe pod <pod-name>	Debug pod issues
kubectl get services	Check exposed services
7. Why This Matters for Your Project
Database persistence: MySQL data survives pod crashes.

Scalability: You can increase replicas in deployment.yaml to handle more traffic.

Zero-downtime updates: Change the Docker image tag → Kubernetes rolls out updates seamlessly.

Production-ready: This same setup works on cloud providers (AWS, GCP, Azure).

